reasoning_details.text:
One failure: "blur valid -> silent". When the user blurs with a valid email, my code calls `paint(state())` which paints "ok" (showing the success message on blur). Is that desirable?

Behavior consideration: showing the success message on blur (before submitting) is a bit premature — the user hasn't subscribed yet. The success message says "Thanks — you're on the list" which asserts something that hasn't happened.

Better: on blur with a valid value, show a neutral confirmation or nothing. Since "no actual submission", showing "Thanks — you're on the list" on blur would be misleading. Let me change blur to paint "idle" when the state is ok (i.e., clear any error but don't claim success).

So:
```js
input.addEventListener("blur", function () {
  if (!input.value.trim()) return;
  var current = state();
  paint(current === "ok" ? "idle" : current);
});
```

That way: blur with an invalid value → error; blur with a valid value → clears the error silently ✓.

